1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,685
Aarav opens an old speaker box.

2
00:00:02,685 --> 00:00:06,316
Inside are two speakers,
a big one and a tiny one.

3
00:00:06,666 --> 00:00:09,292
The tiny one only plays the high notes.

4
00:00:09,292 --> 00:00:12,330
And the only part in
its path is a capacitor.

5
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:15,604
How can one small part
sort sounds by pitch?

6
00:00:16,204 --> 00:00:20,226
A resistor opposes current the
same way at every frequency.

7
00:00:20,575 --> 00:00:23,107
But coils and capacitors are different.

8
00:00:23,107 --> 00:00:25,629
Their opposition changes with frequency.

9
00:00:25,979 --> 00:00:28,352
This opposition is called reactance.

10
00:00:28,352 --> 00:00:31,461
It is measured in ohms,
just like resistance.

11
00:00:32,111 --> 00:00:33,833
Let's see it on the breadboard.

12
00:00:34,183 --> 00:00:39,205
Here is an inductor, a coil of wire,
on an alternating supply of ten volts.

13
00:00:39,555 --> 00:00:43,598
At fifty hertz, about three hundred
and eighteen milliamperes flow.

14
00:00:43,948 --> 00:00:45,737
Now turn the frequency up.

15
00:00:45,737 --> 00:00:47,024
The current falls.

16
00:00:47,374 --> 00:00:51,193
At five hundred hertz, only
about thirty two milliamperes.

17
00:00:51,193 --> 00:00:53,209
The coil fights fast changes.

18
00:00:53,859 --> 00:00:55,474
Look at the scope too.

19
00:00:55,474 --> 00:00:58,792
The current peaks a quarter
cycle after the voltage.

20
00:00:58,792 --> 00:01:02,355
In an inductor, current lags
voltage by ninety degrees.

21
00:01:03,005 --> 00:01:04,750
Now swap in a capacitor.

22
00:01:04,750 --> 00:01:08,428
At fifty hertz, only about
thirty one milliamperes flow.

23
00:01:08,778 --> 00:01:11,615
Turn the frequency up,
and the current rises.

24
00:01:11,615 --> 00:01:14,545
Ten times the frequency,
ten times the current.

25
00:01:14,895 --> 00:01:18,936
And here, the current peaks a
quarter cycle before the voltage.

26
00:01:18,936 --> 00:01:22,520
In a capacitor, current leads
voltage by ninety degrees.

27
00:01:23,170 --> 00:01:24,926
Coils fight fast changes.

28
00:01:24,926 --> 00:01:26,833
Capacitors let them through.

29
00:01:27,633 --> 00:01:29,478
Now let's find the formulas.

30
00:01:29,828 --> 00:01:33,182
A coil makes a back voltage
when its current changes.

31
00:01:33,182 --> 00:01:36,392
v equals L, times the
rate of change of current.

32
00:01:37,092 --> 00:01:41,294
For a sine wave, that rate grows
with omega, which is two pi f.

33
00:01:41,994 --> 00:01:46,030
So the peak voltage is omega
L times the peak current.

34
00:01:46,030 --> 00:01:49,015
This omega L is the inductive reactance.

35
00:01:49,015 --> 00:01:50,994
XL equals two pi f L.

36
00:01:51,694 --> 00:01:53,597
A capacitor stores charge.

37
00:01:53,597 --> 00:01:54,809
q equals C v.

38
00:01:54,809 --> 00:01:59,688
Current is how fast that charge
changes, so i equals C, times the rate

39
00:01:59,688 --> 00:02:01,006
of change of v.

40
00:02:01,706 --> 00:02:03,988
Faster changes push more current.

41
00:02:03,988 --> 00:02:07,732
So the peak current is omega
C times the peak voltage.

42
00:02:08,432 --> 00:02:12,007
The capacitive reactance
is one over omega C.

43
00:02:12,007 --> 00:02:14,814
XC equals one over two pi f C.

44
00:02:15,514 --> 00:02:18,349
L in henry, C in farad, f in hertz.

45
00:02:18,349 --> 00:02:20,688
Both reactances come out in ohms.

46
00:02:21,338 --> 00:02:23,454
Now plot them against frequency.

47
00:02:23,804 --> 00:02:25,512
XL is a straight line.

48
00:02:25,512 --> 00:02:28,287
Double the frequency,
double the reactance.

49
00:02:28,637 --> 00:02:30,552
XC is a curve that falls.

50
00:02:30,552 --> 00:02:33,234
Double the frequency, half the reactance.

51
00:02:33,584 --> 00:02:35,982
For DC, the frequency is zero.

52
00:02:35,982 --> 00:02:41,124
So a coil acts like a plain wire,
and a capacitor blocks DC completely.

53
00:02:41,774 --> 00:02:43,393
Back to the speaker box.

54
00:02:43,393 --> 00:02:46,382
For low notes, the
capacitor's reactance is high.

55
00:02:46,382 --> 00:02:50,008
For high notes it is low, so
they reach the tiny speaker.

56
00:02:50,658 --> 00:02:51,851
Worked example.

57
00:02:51,851 --> 00:02:56,834
A ten microfarad capacitor is connected
to the two hundred and thirty volt,

58
00:02:56,834 --> 00:02:58,250
fifty hertz mains.

59
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:03,481
XC equals one, over two pi, times
fifty, times ten to the minus five.

60
00:03:03,481 --> 00:03:06,469
That is about three
hundred and eighteen ohms.

61
00:03:07,069 --> 00:03:11,845
The current is two hundred and thirty
divided by three hundred and eighteen.

62
00:03:11,845 --> 00:03:14,179
About zero point seven two amperes.

63
00:03:14,829 --> 00:03:16,295
Now, the exam trap.

64
00:03:16,645 --> 00:03:21,615
Many students think every reactance
rises with frequency, like XL does.

65
00:03:21,965 --> 00:03:22,891
Not XC.

66
00:03:22,891 --> 00:03:27,454
It is one over two pi f C, so
it falls as frequency rises.

67
00:03:27,454 --> 00:03:30,031
And for DC, it becomes infinite.

68
00:03:30,681 --> 00:03:32,352
Where is this asked?

69
00:03:32,352 --> 00:03:35,358
In PUC board papers, and in JEE Main.

70
00:03:35,358 --> 00:03:40,810
Find XL or XC, see how the current
changes with frequency, or state the

71
00:03:40,810 --> 00:03:41,710
phase.

72
00:03:42,144 --> 00:03:43,044
Try this.

73
00:03:43,013 --> 00:03:48,088
At fifty hertz, an inductor has a
reactance of forty ohms, and a capacitor

74
00:03:48,088 --> 00:03:51,362
has a reactance of one
hundred and sixty ohms.

75
00:03:51,362 --> 00:03:54,731
At what frequency will
their reactances be equal?

76
00:04:00,131 --> 00:04:01,031
B.

77
00:04:00,621 --> 00:04:02,209
One hundred hertz.

78
00:04:02,559 --> 00:04:04,446
XL grows with frequency.

79
00:04:04,446 --> 00:04:07,474
At f, it is forty, times f over fifty.

80
00:04:07,824 --> 00:04:09,667
XC falls with frequency.

81
00:04:09,667 --> 00:04:13,631
At f, it is one hundred and
sixty, times fifty over f.

82
00:04:13,981 --> 00:04:15,187
Set them equal.

83
00:04:15,187 --> 00:04:19,662
f squared is four times fifty
squared, so f is one hundred hertz.

84
00:04:20,312 --> 00:04:21,836
Screenshot this card.

85
00:04:25,036 --> 00:04:27,001
Next, impedance and resonance.

86
00:04:27,001 --> 00:04:30,630
What happens when a coil and
a capacitor share one circuit?
